<article xml:id="document" label="document">
  <title>New LaTeX-style PreTeXt document</title>

  <theorem>
    <title>3.1.3. Point to line distance</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        The (shortest) distance between a point and a line is from the point to
        the foot of the perpendicular.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <proof>
    <p>
      Consider line <m>\overleftrightarrow{AB}</m> and a point <m>P</m> not on
      the line. Let point <m>X</m> be the foot of the perpendicular from point
      <m>P</m> to line <m>\overleftrightarrow{AB}</m>.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <image source="asset-3-1"/>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      For the sake of contradiction, suppose <m>|PX|</m> is not the shortest
      distance from <m>P</m> to <m>\overleftrightarrow{AB}</m>. Then, there
      exists a point <m>Y</m> on <m>\overleftrightarrow{AB}</m> such that
      <m>|PY|&lt;|PX|</m>. By the Ruler Postulate, we can extend
      <m>\overline{PX}</m> and place a point <m>P'</m> on <m>\overline{PX}</m>
      such that <m>\overline{PX}\cong\overline{P'X}</m>. Then, by axiom 1, we
      can create segment <m>\overline{P'Y}</m>. Since <m>\angle YXP</m> is a
      right angle, so is <m>\angle YXP'</m> by the Supplement Postulate.
      Further, <m>\overline{XY}\cong\overline{XY}</m> by reflexivity. Thus, by
      the SAS Postulate, <m>\triangle PXY\cong\triangle P'XY</m>. So,
      <m>\overline{PY}\cong\overline{P'Y}</m>. By the Triangle Inequality,
      <md>
        |PY|+|P'Y|&gt;|PP'|.
      </md>
      But, <m>\overline{PY}\cong\overline{P'Y}</m> and <m>|PP'|=2|PX|</m> since
      <m>\overline{PX}\cong\overline{P'X}</m>. Thus,
      <md>
        2|PY|&gt;2|PX|\implies |PX|&gt;|PX|,
      </md>
      which is a contradiction. Hence, <m>|PX|</m> must be the shortest distance
      from point <m>P</m> to <m>\overleftrightarrow{AB}</m>.
    </p>
  </proof>

  <theorem>
    <title>3.1.8</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Playfair and the alternating interior angle converse theorem imply the
        equidistance of parallel lines.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <proof>
    <p>
      Let <m>P</m> be a point not on line <m>\ell_{1}</m>. Then, by Playfair,
      there exists exactly one line <m>\ell_{2}</m> such that <m>P</m> lies in
      <m>\ell_{2}</m> and <m>\ell_{1}\parallel\ell_{2}</m>. Choose two arbitrary
      points <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> on <m>\ell_{2}</m>. By Theorem 3.1.3, the
      closest distance from <m>A</m> to <m>\ell_{1}</m> is from <m>A</m> to the
      foot of the perpendicular. Let <m>C</m> be the point at the foot of the
      perpendicular on <m>\ell_{1}</m> from <m>A</m>. Similarly, let <m>D</m> be
      the point at the foot of the perpendicular on <m>\ell_{1}</m> when finding
      the distance from <m>B</m> to <m>\ell_{1}</m>.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <image width="75%" source="asset-3-1-3"/>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      We claim that <m>\overline{AC}\cong\overline{BD}</m>. Since
      <m>\ell_{1}\parallel\ell_{2}</m>, by the alternating interior angle
      converse theorem, the angles at the intersection of <m>\overline{AC}</m>
      and <m>\ell_{2}</m> are right angles and same with <m>\overline{BD}</m>.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <image width="75%" source="asset-3-1-4"/>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      By axiom 1, we can construct segment <m>\overline{BC}</m>. Then, by the
      alternating interior angle converse theorem,
      <m>\angle ABC\cong\angle DCB</m>.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <image width="75%" source="asset-3-1-5"/>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      By reflexivity, <m>\overline{BC}</m> is congruent to itself. So, we have
      that
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\angle ABC\cong\angle DCB</m>,
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\angle CAB\cong\angle BDC</m>,
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\overline{BC}\cong\overline{BC}</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      By Angle-Angle-Side, <m>\triangle ABC\cong\triangle DCB</m>, making
      <m>\overline{AC}\cong\overline{BD}</m> as desired. Since <m>A</m> and
      <m>B</m> were arbitrary points, parallel lines <m>\ell_{1}</m> and
      <m>\ell_{2}</m> are everywhere equidistant.
    </p>
  </proof>

  <theorem>
    <title>3.1.9</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        The equidistance of parallel lines implies Euclid's postulate.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <proof>
    <p>
      Let <m>\ell_{1}</m> and <m>\ell_{2}</m> be a pair of lines with a
      transversal <m>t</m> that intersects <m>\ell_{1}</m> at point <m>t_{1}</m>
      and <m>\ell_{2}</m> at point <m>t_{2}</m> such that the sum of the angles
      on one side of <m>t</m>, say <m>\angle1</m> and <m>\angle2</m>, is less
      than two right angles. We claim that <m>\ell_{1}</m> and <m>\ell_{2}</m>
      intersect on the same side of the transversal as <m>\angle1</m> and
      <m>\angle2</m>.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <image width="75%" source="asset-3-1-6"/>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      Assume the contrary, that is <m>\ell_{1}</m> and <m>\ell_{2}</m> do not
      intersect on the same side of <m>t</m> as <m>\angle1</m> and
      <m>\angle2</m>. Then, we have two cases: <m>\ell_{1}\parallel\ell_{2}</m>
      or <m>\ell_{1}</m> intersects with <m>\ell_{2}</m> on the other side of
      <m>t</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      <alert>Case 1: <m>\ell_{1}\parallel\ell_{2}</m>:</alert> Since parallel
      lines are equidistant, assume the distance between <m>\ell_{1}</m> and
      <m>\ell_{2}</m> is <m>d&gt;0</m>. Here, we will explore two subcases.
    </p>

    <p>
      <alert>Subcase 1.1: <m>m\angle1&lt;\pi/2</m> and <m>m\angle2&lt;\pi/2</m>:</alert>
      Then, we know that <m>|t_{1}t_{2}|</m> is not the shortest distance from
      <m>t_{1}</m> to <m>\ell_{2}</m> and <m>t_{2}</m> to <m>\ell_{1}</m> by
      Theorem 3.1.3. So, draw the distance lines from each point to the opposing
      line.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <image width="75%" source="asset-3-1-7"/>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      If <m>\ell_{1}\parallel\ell_{2}</m>, we know that
      <m>|t_{1}a_{2}|=|t_{2}a_{1}|=d</m> since parallel lines are equidistant.
      By reflexivity, <m>\overline{t_1t_2}\cong\overline{t_2t_1}</m>. Further,
      by Theorem 3.1.3 we know that
      <m>m\angle t_{1}a_{2}t_{2}=m\angle t_{2}a_{1}t_{1}=\pi/2</m>. So, by Right
      Angle-Side-Side,
      <m>\triangle t_{1}a_{2}t_{2}\cong\triangle t_{1}a_{1}t_{2}</m> and
      therefore <m>\angle 1\cong \angle t_{1}t_{2}a_{2}</m>. By the Supplement
      Postulate,
      <md>
        m\angle2+m\angle t_{1}t_{2}a_{2}=\pi\implies m\angle2+m\angle1=\pi,
      </md>
      which is a contradiction since we are assuming
      <m>m\angle1+m\angle2&lt;\pi</m>. Note that the same reasoning would apply
      if instead <m>\angle 2</m> was an interior angle to a triangle instead of
      <m>\angle 1</m> (that is, if instead <m>t</m> crossed <m>\ell_{1}</m>
      first when going from left to right or if <m>\angle 1</m> and
      <m>\angle 2</m> were on the other side of <m>t</m> in the figure.)
    </p>

    <p>
      <alert>Subcase 1.2: <m>m\angle1=\pi/2</m> and <m>m\angle2&lt;\pi/2</m>:</alert>
      Then, <m>\overline{t_1t_2}</m> is the shortest distance from <m>t_{2}</m>
      to <m>\ell_{1}</m> by Theorem 3.1.3 and thus <m>|t_{1}t_{2}|=d</m>. Now,
      we have even more cases. If the distance line from <m>t_{1}</m> to
      <m>\ell_{2}</m> is on the same side of <m>t</m> as <m>\angle 2</m>, then
      we have the following figure.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <image width="75%" source="asset-3-1-8"/>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      Since <m>\ell_{1}\parallel\ell_{2}</m>, <m>|t_{1}c|=d</m> as well. Thus,
      by the property of isosceles triangles (Theorem 2.1.8)
      <m>m\angle2=\pi/2</m>, which is a contradiction. If instead the distance
      line from <m>t_{1}</m> to <m>\ell_{2}</m> is on the other side of
      <m>t</m>, then we have the following figure.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <image width="75%" source="asset-3-1-9.png"/>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      Again, by the properties of isosceles triangles
      <m>m\angle t_{1}t_{2}b=\pi/2</m> and by the supplement postulate
      <m>m\angle2=\pi/2</m>, which is a contradiction. A similar reasoning
      applies when instead <m>m\angle1&lt;\pi/2</m> and <m>m\angle2=\pi/2</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      <alert>Case 2: <m>\ell_{1}</m> and <m>\ell_{2}</m> intersect on the other side of <m>t</m>:</alert>
      Suppose <m>\ell_{1}</m> and <m>\ell_{2}</m> intersect at a point <m>X</m>
      on the other side of <m>t</m>.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <image width="75%" source="asset-3-1-10"/>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      Then, by the Supplement Postulate,
      <md alignment="align">
        <mrow>m\angle3&amp;=\pi-m\angle1,</mrow>
        <mrow>m\angle4&amp;=\pi-m\angle2.</mrow>
      </md>
      Further, by the Exterior Angle Theorem,
      <md alignment="align">
        <mrow>m\angle1&gt;m\angle4&amp;\implies m\angle1&gt;\pi-m\angle 2,</mrow>
        <mrow>m\angle2&gt;m\angle3&amp;\implies m\angle2&gt;\pi-m\angle 1.</mrow>
      </md>
      Adding the inequalities, we get
      <md alignment="align">
        <mrow>m\angle1+m\angle2&amp;&gt;2\pi-m\angle1-m\angle2,</mrow>
        <mrow>\implies 2m\angle1+2m\angle2&amp;&gt;2\pi,</mrow>
        <mrow>\implies m\angle1+m\angle2&amp;&gt;\pi,</mrow>
      </md>
      which is a contradiction. Hence, if <m>m\angle1+m\angle2&lt;\pi</m>, then
      <m>\ell_{1}</m> and <m>\ell_{2}</m> must intersect on the same side of
      <m>t</m> as <m>\angle1</m> and <m>\angle2</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      One should notice that in the process of proving this theorem, we showed
      that if two lines are parallel, then the angles on the same side of a
      transversal between the lines sum up to two right angles. This is the
      converse of Theorem 3.1.4
    </p>
  </proof>
</article>
</pretext>